---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const images = `${base}images/remote-holder/`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Remote Holder">
  <header class="page-intro project-intro">
    <a href={`${base}work/`}>Back to work</a>
    <p class="eyebrow">Design study / Reverse engineering</p>
    <h1>Remote Holder</h1>
    <p class="intro muted">Remotes of different shapes, and a side table they were always loose on. Scanning both the remotes and the table gave me the geometry for a holder that fits all of it.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="project-opening" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
    <figure>
      <img src={`${images}holder-cad.jpg`} width="1400" height="1046" alt="Render of the holder model: a block with pockets of different shapes cut into its sloped upper face." fetchpriority="high" />
      <figcaption>The finished body. Render of the model.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="project-goal">
      <h2 id="problem-heading">Different shapes, one holder</h2>
      <p>No two of the remotes share a shape. Anything generic enough to hold all of them would hold none of them properly.</p>
      <p>That made it a fitment problem rather than a styling one, and fitment problems are answered by capturing the real geometry rather than guessing at it.</p>
      <dl class="project-facts">
        <div><dt>Process</dt><dd>3D scanning, mesh cleanup, CAD, 3D printing</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Scanner</dt><dd>Revopoint POP 4, turntable and global markers</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Scanned</dt><dd>Each remote, and the side table it sits on</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Result</dt><dd>A single holder with a pocket per remote</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section text-step" aria-labelledby="capture-heading">
    <div class="process-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 / Capture</p>
      <h2 id="capture-heading">Two scans doing two different jobs</h2>
      <p>Every dimension in the holder came off a scan. I scanned each remote to get its outline and depth, and separately scanned the side table to get the surface the holder would sit on. The remotes define the pockets. The table defines the base.</p>
      <p>Scanning the environment rather than only the object is the part that made this different from the vacuum dock. A part can fit its contents perfectly and still sit badly where it lives.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section text-step" aria-labelledby="test-heading">
    <div class="process-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">02 / Test the fit first</p>
      <h2 id="test-heading">One pocket before all of them</h2>
      <p>Before committing to the full body I modelled a flat test coupon carrying a single pocket, so a wrong clearance would cost one small print instead of the whole holder.</p>
      <p>The coupon is 102 by 57 by 24 mm, which is enough material around the pocket to check the fit without printing anything that has to look like the final part.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section text-step" aria-labelledby="holder-heading">
    <div class="process-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">03 / The holder</p>
      <h2 id="holder-heading">A pocket shaped to each remote</h2>
      <p>The finished body is 140 by 97 by 62 mm, with a pocket cut to the shape of each remote.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="project-end"><a href={`${base}work/`}>Back to all work</a></div>
</BaseLayout>
